// @emit-error bare null needs a struct-typed context
// An INLINE `m[k] == null` over a LIST-valued map has no arm in the null-test ladder, and
// THE VALUE HERE IS NOT NULLABLE — which is the whole point of pinning it.
//
// It is the control that keeps the nullable-list-value niche
// (`maps/nullable-list-valued-map.vl`) from being blamed for it. That niche is closed: every
// op there lowers, including the read bound first (`const r = m[k]; if r != null`), which is
// the same question asked one step later. What has no arm is the UNBOUND map-read receiver at
// the null test for a list VALUE — a `{[string]: S}` struct value answers it
// (`exprNullableStruct`'s Index arm reads `mapReadMvSlot`), and the list classifiers
// (`exprNullableList` / `exprNulScalarListKind` / `exprNullableRefArray`) carry an Index arm
// for a `(T[] | null)[]` ELEMENT read only, never for a map read.
//
// So this is a missing arm on the LIST family's map-read rung, orthogonal to nullability, and
// it is loud — never invalid wasm. Delete this file when that rung lands, not before: while it
// stands, a claim that the nullable-list map value is still broken can be checked against it in
// one command.
function go() {
  const m: {[string]: f64[]} = Map()
  m["k"] = [1.5]
  print(m["k"] == null)
}
go()
